- Online target `vhabpcoyypobgasacsko`: not inspected yet (T8 does a read-only check; T15 the full preflight).
- None of these contradicts the SPEC.

## Block C — AI contracts, intake, and provider access

## T9 — Usage accounting, admission, processing tickets, and idempotency contracts
Reasoning: Cost protection must hold under concurrency and replay before any provider call exists; this task owns what 
the database and ticket code can prove on their own (review P5). Dispatch-dependent evidence moves to T12. T8 (TRACK): 
settings table `public.meeting_note_ai_settings` (company_id PK, revision, daily_limit; no row = limit 20, not 
configured); key only via `public.read_meeting_note_ai_key(p_company_id)` (service_role; NULL = not configured) and 
Vault secret `meeting_notes_ai_key:<company_id>`; admission must lock the settings row after the actor users row (FOR 
KEY SHARE) and before the note row, and return the key + snapshot from the same transaction.
Task: Server-only usage records per company per day (America/Sao_Paulo). One `service_role`-only admission RPC that, 
in a single transaction holding the company settings row lock: (1) verifies the caller's current membership and editor 
rights on the note (for replays too); (2) looks up an existing operation for the ticket's idempotency key — if found 
with the same binding, returns its status without new admission, regardless of later settings or source changes; if 
found with a different binding, rejects; (3) otherwise, with the note row locked, verifies that the bound settings 
revision is current and that the note's revision and transcript identity equal the ticket's (stale source → conflict, 
nothing reserved), reserves usage against the limit, records the operation with the provider/model/effort snapshot, 
and returns the matching key and the bound transcript text from the same snapshot. Settlement once, against the 
admission day, by a `service_role`-only RPC; it counts every dispatched operation, refunds only pre-processing 
rejections, and releases the reservation (not counted) for an operation that demonstrably never sent a request (size 
preflight refusal, cancellation before send, error before dispatch); settlement is idempotent. The idempotency key is 
generated by the server at ticket issuance and embedded in the signed ticket (a client cannot choose a key or reuse 
one with a new ticket); the key expires with its ticket, 24 h after issuance, checked before any lookup or admission. 
Idempotency keys scoped to user+company+operation and bound to note id, source revision, transcript identity, settings 
revision. Signed processing tickets (issue time, idempotency key, user, company, note, source revision, transcript 
identity, settings revision) with HMAC-SHA256 using the required server-only env var `MEETING_NOTES_TICKET_SECRET` 
(≥32 bytes, no fallback — AI start refuses with a configuration error when missing), plus optional 
`MEETING_NOTES_TICKET_SECRET_PREVIOUS` accepted for verification only so a rotation keeps outstanding tickets valid 
for their 24 h; the local value is provisioned in `.env.local` and `.env.example` documents it. A ticket-issue route 
(called when the notice is shown) authorizes current membership and editor rights before signing. Operation records 
without content kept 30 days (pg_cron cleanup); an operation without outcome 10 minutes after dispatch becomes 
"failed" and stays counted (pg_cron); deleting notes or users never changes usage; no client role can read-modify 
usage beyond reading its own company's count.
Done when: Admission, settlement, replay lookup, and ticket issuance satisfy FR-022/FR-023/FR-031 at the database and 
route boundary, independently of any provider.
Verify by: Integration tests: five concurrent admissions at 19/20 → exactly one reserved, four refused (AC-028); same 
key + binding returns the existing operation across midnight; different binding rejected; direct 
`anon`/`authenticated` calls to admission, settlement, and usage tables denied (AC-028); a settings save racing an 
admission yields either admission under the old revision with the old snapshot or refusal — never a mixed snapshot 
(AC-025 DB side); a transcript replaced after ticket issuance makes the new admission a conflict with nothing 
reserved; a released never-dispatched reservation leaves usage unchanged and a second settlement changes nothing; the 
stale-operation job turns an operation backdated >10 min into "failed", still counted; the cleanup job removes records 
older than 30 days; note deletion leaves usage unchanged (AC-034 DB side); limit-reached response data and reset at 
00:00 America/Sao_Paulo (AC-016 DB side); ticket-issue route denies a removed member and a non-editor and embeds a 
server-generated key; `supabase-rls-reviewer` audit.
Covers: FR-022, FR-023, FR-031, BR-008, AC-016, AC-025, AC-028, AC-034
Root: T9
Depends on: T8

## T10 — Transcript intake: upload, attach, validation, normalization
Reasoning: The transcript must be validated and stored before any AI step (owner r4: saved at upload). T17's 
`create_meeting_note` requires a space of the caller's company and does not take a transcript; transcript writes need 
their own revision-checked, versioned path under the note row lock.
Task: Routes for "New note from transcript" (creates the note with title = file name ≤200, date = today, transcript 
stored, version created) and "Attach transcript" (editors only; replaces the stored transcript and transcript identity 
immediately; summary untouched; revision bumped). Files must be .txt/.vtt/.srt, non-empty, ≤1 MB, valid UTF-8 text; 
otherwise rejected with a message and no change. A server normalizer removes VTT/SRT cue numbers, timestamps, and cue 
settings while keeping speaker names and text; it is the single source of the text sent to providers (its 
received-text evidence is T12's).
Done when: Valid transcripts create or update notes; invalid ones change nothing.
Verify by: Integration tests through the routes for AC-010 (upload creates note immediately; attach replaces, summary 
unchanged), AC-011 (each invalid file rejected, no note created or changed, usage unchanged), AC-024; 
`supabase-rls-reviewer` audit.
Covers: FR-012, FR-013, FR-014, BR-010, AC-010, AC-011, AC-024
Root: T10
Depends on: T17, T3

## T16 — Local fake AI server harness
Reasoning: Every AI test needs a deterministic stand-in for four provider wire protocols; separating it from the 
adapters gives it its own checkpoint (review P11).
Task: A test-only fake AI server under the meeting-notes test tree that speaks the Anthropic Messages API and the 
OpenAI Chat Completions/Responses shapes used by OpenAI, Kimi, and GLM; records every received request (headers 
without keys, body); and scripts outcomes per test: success with a given draft, error after processing, timeout, 
refusal, malformed JSON, each listed pre-processing rejection, unlisted statuses, overload after streaming began, 
connection drop after acceptance, and pause/resume. The DB integration suite's setup and the E2E runner start and stop 
it on a fixed local port.
Done when: Both test runners start the fake server, it answers each protocol with the scripted outcome, records 
requests, and shuts down cleanly.
Verify by: The meeting-notes DB suite setup starts it and a setup self-check sends one request per protocol and reads 
back the recorded request; the E2E config starts and stops it (config inspected; executed at T14).
Covers: AC-026, AC-029
Root: T16
Depends on: —

## T11 — Provider adapters
Reasoning: Four providers with different wire protocols must produce the same draft schema, never retry silently, and 
classify rejections only by documented signals. T8 (TRACK) capability matrix 
`src/lib/meeting-notes/ai/capabilities.ts` holds models, reasoning wire fragments, budgets, base URLs (Kimi 
api.moonshot.ai, GLM api.z.ai), and rejection combos; GLM supports only `json_object`, so its draft must be validated 
after parsing.
Task: Server-only adapters: Anthropic via `@anthropic-ai/sdk`, OpenAI via `openai`, Kimi and GLM via the OpenAI SDK 
with their OpenAI-compatible base URLs; SDK retries disabled (`maxRetries: 0`); each sends only the matrix's valid 
parameters for the chosen model and reasoning level; output requested and validated against one draft schema (summary 
sections, ≤100 items with description and assignee name as written); malformed/truncated output is a failure; an 
input-size preflight against the model's context/output budget refuses oversize transcripts before any request; 
provider errors are mapped to sanitized messages (no key, no transcript) and classified as pre-processing rejection 
only per the matrix. A server-only base-URL override selects the fake server; it is ignored and logged as refused when 
`NODE_ENV=production`, and no client input can set it.
Done when: Each adapter, called by server code against the fake server over HTTP, returns a validated draft or a 
correctly classified failure, with exactly one outbound request per call.
Verify by: Integration tests against the T16 server: per adapter, received parameters match the matrix and a draft is 
returned (AC-026); oversize transcript → zero received requests (AC-026); connection drop → exactly one received 
request; listed rejections classified pre-processing; unlisted/mid-stream classified counted (AC-029 classification); 
override ignored under `NODE_ENV=production`. Usage effects of these outcomes are T12's evidence.
Covers: FR-016, FR-020, AC-026, AC-029
Root: T11
Depends on: T8, T16

## Block D — AI processing flow and UI

## T12 — Processing and Apply flow on the server
Reasoning: Joins admission, dispatch, draft, authorization rechecks, and atomic Apply — the heart of FR-015–FR-021 — 
and owns every dispatch-dependent check (review P2–P5). Apply appends items as T2 (TRACK) established: call 
`private.meeting_note_lock_for_change(note_id, <NOT_FOUND token>)` (actor KEY SHARE → note FOR UPDATE; returns 
can_edit, note_revision), insert with creator snapshots and `assignee_name` whenever `assignee_id` is set; the item 
insert trigger raises 22023 MEETING_NOTE_ITEM_LIMIT_REACHED past 100 and the whole Apply rolls back; READ COMMITTED.
Task: A start route that, in order: verifies the ticket signature and the 24 h expiry of the ticket and its embedded 
key before any lookup (expired → rejected, no dispatch, even without records; a client-supplied key differing from the 
ticket's is rejected); compares the ticket's user/company/note with the authenticated application user; calls T9's 
admission RPC, which re-authorizes current membership and editor rights before anything else (existing operation → 
returns its pending/final/"discarded" status without dispatch, even if settings changed since; new operation → current 
membership, editor rights, and current settings revision required, stale revision → refused with the new provider 
name); runs T11's size preflight and, when it refuses or the client cancels before sending, settles the operation as 
never dispatched (released); otherwise dispatches once via T11 with the admitted snapshot, key, and transcript; 
resolves suggested names locally (exact full name, else unique first name, case/accent-insensitive, current members 
only); rechecks membership and note existence before returning; honors client abort (operation "discarded") with 
best-effort outbound abort; and settles usage. An `authenticated`-granted Apply RPC accepts the edited summary and 
accepted items only at the draft's source revision, rechecks rights in the transaction, marks suggestions duplicating 
existing items, appends accepted items under T2's shared per-note lock and capacity, writes the summary, and creates 
one version — or changes nothing.
Done when: Processing and Apply satisfy FR-015–FR-021 against the fake provider, including every dispatch-dependent 
usage, replay, and authorization outcome.
Verify by: Integration tests through the routes against local Supabase and the T16 server for AC-010 (draft changes 
nothing until Apply; Apply stores exactly edited summary and accepted items, one version), AC-012 (text received by 
the fake provider for .vtt/.srt has no cue numbers/timestamps/settings and keeps speakers), AC-013 (name matching; 
request contains no roster), AC-016 (each failure outcome's counting), AC-025 (stale notice refused; admitted 
operation completes on P), AC-026 (oversize refused, zero received requests, usage unchanged; cancellation before send 
releases the reservation), AC-027, AC-028 (concurrent starts dispatch once; replay received once), AC-029 (connection 
drop counted once; listed rejections not counted; unlisted/mid-stream counted), AC-030 (paused provider with 
transcript replacement / member removal), AC-032 (98 items: add vs two-item Apply; a settings save, an admission, and 
an Apply by that admin running concurrently on the same note all finish without a deadlock error), AC-034 
(cancelled-then-completed replay → "discarded"; expired ticket after record cleanup and with records retained; a fresh 
ticket cannot reuse an earlier key; replay after a settings change returns the original operation; a child process 
running the start route is killed after the fake provider records receipt, the operation's dispatch time is then set 
11 minutes in the past in the local database, and running the stale-operation job yields "failed", counted — no real 
10-minute wait, within the suite timeout; a ticket issued before a restart of that child process is still accepted), 
member removed or demoted before start → zero dispatches and usage unchanged; AC-023 cross-company matrix: a member 
and an admin of each company attempt every meeting-note, item, transcript, search, settings, ticket, processing, 
Apply, and usage route plus direct table/RPC access on the other company — all denied or empty; 
`supabase-rls-reviewer` audit.
Covers: FR-014, FR-015, FR-016, FR-017, FR-018, FR-019, FR-021, FR-022, FR-031, BR-006, BR-008, BR-013, AC-010, 
AC-012, AC-013, AC-016, AC-023, AC-025, AC-026, AC-027, AC-028, AC-029, AC-030, AC-032, AC-034
