# OpenAI Docs

Provide current, cited OpenAI product, API, model, and Codex guidance. Read zero or one primary reference.

**First substantive action:** Search the user's exact requested official OpenAI documentation topic and any explicitly 
named model using a concise, topic-specific query of 2-6 essential terms. When an already-available direct official 
documentation search and page-retrieval capability is present, use it first: search, then fetch or open the matching 
official page before general web search. Otherwise, immediately use official-domain web search, then actually open or 
fetch the relevant official page. Complete this source order before reading a reference, inspecting local or 
repository files, running a Codex manual or model resolver, drafting a plan, or answering from memory. Use the actual 
fetched page, not a search snippet or an unopened link. If one official search or page does not establish the answer, 
search another appropriate official domain and actually open or fetch the result. Preserve the exact requested model; 
never substitute a newer model.

**Only exception:** An explicitly requested, genuinely broad, cross-topic Codex setup, orientation, or system-map 
synthesis may use the manual first when shell execution and an allowed temporary cache are available. A specific Codex 
feature, setting, command, error, model, or requested citation remains docs-first. Mixed Chat/Work/Codex comparisons 
are official documentation questions, not manual-first Codex requests.

For generic software tasks, answer the software task directly. OpenAI implementation, debugging, SDK, API, prompting, 
agent, and eval requests are not generic.

For a straightforward factual or citation-only request, follow the source order and do not read a route reference. 
This includes straightforward API facts, ChatGPT Work or mixed Chat/Work/Codex comparisons, model tiers, aliases, Pro 
mode, reasoning settings, factual migration baselines, and narrow Codex facts. Prioritize `learn.chatgpt.com` for 
ChatGPT Work.

## Choose one primary route

Use the first matching route, and read its reference only when the requested task needs that specialized workflow:

- **Explicitly requested local documentation integration:** Read [integration guidance](references/mcp-diagnostics.md) 
only when the user explicitly requests that local integration.
- **Model migration, upgrades, or model-specific prompting:** Read [model-migration.md](references/model-migration.md) 
for actual migration planning, implementation, dynamic target resolution, or prompt changes. Preserve an explicitly 
requested target.
- **Model selection and comparisons:** Read [model-selection.md](references/model-selection.md) only when nuanced 
current, latest, default, cost, latency, quality, or modality tradeoffs need more guidance. Do not run a migration 
resolver for selection alone.
- **Product, API, ChatGPT Work, and mixed Chat/Work/Codex documentation:** Read 
[official-docs.md](references/official-docs.md) only when fetched official pages leave source selection, API schemas, 
or the requested implementation unresolved. This route is not manual-first.
- **Explicitly broad Codex setup, orientation, or cross-topic synthesis:** Read 
[codex-self-knowledge.md](references/codex-self-knowledge.md) when the eligible Codex manual or deeper Codex 
procedures are needed.

Read at most one primary reference. Do not open every route, bundled model guide, or helper script. Read a supporting 
reference or run a helper only when the chosen workflow demonstrably needs it.

## Source and execution boundaries

- Search, open, fetch, and cite only `developers.openai.com`, `platform.openai.com`, and `learn.chatgpt.com`. Cite the 
page that supports the claim. State uncertainty when official sources do not establish pricing, availability, account 
access, limits, or behavior.
- Preserve an explicitly requested model for selection, migration, and prompting. Resolve an unspecified latest or 
current migration target only after searching and fetching current official guidance.
- Use `references/latest-model.md` only as a disclosed fallback after current official model guidance does not answer 
the question. Read `references/upgrading-to-gpt-6-astra.md` only for an actual, requested GPT-6 migration; read 
`references/prompting-guide.md` only for requested prompting work.
- Before building, running, editing, debugging, or testing an API-backed app or tool, use `openai-platform-api-key` 
first when available. Documentation, conceptual examples, model selection, and read-only guidance do not require an 
API key.
- Say "OpenAI Docs" or "official OpenAI documentation" in user-facing answers. Keep exact official citations and 
examples concise.
135:
136:| ID | Rule | Rationale |
137:|---|---|---|
138:| BR-001 | Membership means the user's current `users.company_id` equals the note's company. All members can view 
all of their company's notes, versions, participants, action items, and transcripts; nobody else can. | Notes are 
company memory (r1 q4). |
139:| BR-002 | Any member can create a note in any space of their company, including private spaces. | r2 q11. |
140:| BR-003 | Edit rights: the author (while a member), current participants who are members, and company admins. 
Delete rights: the author (while a member) and company admins. Rights are evaluated on the state before the change, at 
the API and at the database. | r1 q5, r2 q11; prevents self-grant. |
141:| BR-004 | Editors may change the participant list, which changes who can edit; a viewer cannot add themselves; 
restore restores the version's participants under the same pre-change authorization. | Participants are versioned (r2 
q10). |
142:| BR-005 | Action items: editors create, edit, delete; editors and the current assignee toggle completion; nobody 
changes an item's note, creator, or creation time. | r2 q8. |
143:| BR-006 | AI suggestions never become content or assignments without explicit user Apply. | r1 q12. |
144:| BR-007 | Each accepted change creates one immutable version; restore creates a new version; action items and 
transcript are current-state only. | r1 q8, r2 q10. |
145:| BR-008 | The daily limit applies to every company, counts dispatched processings as in FR-022 (reprocessing 
included), defaults to 20, is adjustable by admins from 1 to 200, and resets at midnight America/Sao_Paulo. | r1 q11, 
r2 q4, r4. |
146:| BR-009 | Only admins configure AI. The API key is stored encrypted at rest in storage that no browser session 
can select, subscribe to, or decrypt; it is never stored in `companies.settings` or any client-readable table. | r2 
q1, q3; review M6. |
147:| BR-010 | One transcript per note; a new upload replaces it immediately; the stored transcript is visible to 
every viewer of the note. | r1 q13, r2 q12, r4. |
148:| BR-011 | A change based on an outdated note revision is rejected. | r1 q18. |
149:| BR-012 | AI access uses API keys only; no subscription credentials are requested, stored, or proxied. | 
Anthropic policy; r3. |
150:| BR-013 | A processing uses the settings snapshot taken at admission; settings changes affect only later 
admissions; no silent fallback between providers. | Privacy notice fidelity; review H4. |
151:
152:## 9. Data and integrations
153:
154:### Data inputs
155:
156:- Note fields, participants, rich-text content, action items, transcript files (.txt/.vtt/.srt, UTF-8 text, ≤1 MB).
157:- Admin AI settings: provider, API key, model, reasoning level, daily limit.
158:
159:### Data outputs
160:
161:- Stored notes, versions, participants, action items, transcripts, AI settings metadata, encrypted keys, usage 
records.
162:- AI drafts (transient until applied).
163:- Search results with excerpts; downloaded .md files.
164:
165:### Integrations
166:
167:- Anthropic Messages API (official SDK), OpenAI API (official SDK), Kimi (Moonshot) and GLM (Zhipu/Z.ai) through 
their OpenAI-compatible endpoints; each adapter sends only parameters valid for its provider and model (curated 
capability matrix). All calls are server-side from authenticated API routes.
271:- AC-017 (FR-024, BR-009): Given an admin, when they save provider, key, model, reasoning level, and limit 50, 
then the settings persist and the key is shown only as its last four characters; a canary key string never appears in 
any API response, company read, direct Supabase select (any role), Realtime payload, server log line, or client 
bundle; when a member calls the settings route, then it is denied; limit 0 or 201 is rejected; a company that never 
saved settings has limit 20.
272:- AC-018 (FR-025): Given no provider configured, when a member opens a note, then manual editing works and AI 
controls are disabled with "An admin needs to configure AI"; an admin additionally sees a link to settings.
273:- AC-019 (FR-005, FR-027): Given a note with formatted content, summary, items, and transcript, when exported 
without the option, then the .md contains title, date, space, author, participants, Markdown content, summary, and 
items but no transcript; with the option ticked, the transcript is included.
274:- AC-020 (FR-028, FR-029): Given a space with notes, when the space is deleted, then its notes remain on the 
Meetings page and in search labelled "Space removed", keep their author and company, and remain editable by the same 
people; when a company is deleted, then its notes are deleted.
275:- AC-021 (FR-030): Given a participant and assignee who leaves the company, then the note shows "Former member" 
with the snapshot name and that user can no longer read the note; when the user row is deleted, then the snapshot name 
still shows; when they rejoin the same company, then their participant rights return; saving the note while a former 
member is still listed succeeds.
276:- AC-022 (SM-1, BR-012): Given the owner's OpenAI API key configured on the new app against the online database, 
when processing the one-hour fixture transcript, then the draft appears within 60 seconds; at no point is a 
subscription login requested.
277:- AC-023 (SM-3, BR-001, BR-009, FR-029): Given two companies, when a member and an admin of each attempt direct 
API and database access to the other's notes, versions, participants, items, transcripts, search, AI settings, keys, 
and usage, then every attempt returns nothing or is denied, and `company_id`/`created_by` cannot be changed by any 
client.
278:- AC-024 (FR-012): Given an existing note, when a non-editor tries to attach a transcript, then it is denied and 
the stored transcript is unchanged.
1:# PLAN: phase-6-meeting-notes
2:
3:Spec: ./SPEC.md
4:Goal: Company members can record, revise, search, and export meeting notes per space — with action items, version 
history, and an AI-generated draft summary and action items from an uploaded transcript using the company's own AI 
provider key.
5:Plan version: 4
6:Highest task ID reserved: T17
7:Replanned because: the pre-execution adversarial PLAN review r3 (`reviews/sol-review-plan-r3.md`) found that 
unspecified row-lock modes and order across the settings save, admission, Apply, and version inserts permit a deadlock 
(R3-1). No task has been executed.
8:
9:## Standing rules for every task
10:
11:- **Migration rule (SPEC §9, §10):** a migration is done when it is written, applied to the LOCAL database, its 
integration tests pass, and its rollback SQL is recorded in the migration header and TRACK. Each gets a row in 
`spec-interview/phase-6-meeting-notes/MIGRATIONS-PENDENTES.md`. Nothing is applied online before T15.
12:- **Function privilege rule (review P1):** the baseline default privileges grant EXECUTE on every new `public` 
function to `anon`, `authenticated`, and `service_role` (`20260610183000_baseline_core_schema.sql:2131-2134`). Every 
new function therefore: has owner `postgres`, `SET search_path = ''` with schema-qualified references, `REVOKE ALL ... 
FROM PUBLIC, anon, authenticated, service_role`, and then an explicit `GRANT EXECUTE` only to the role that needs it; 
integration tests read the effective EXECUTE matrix from the catalog. User-facing RPCs that authorize the caller 
themselves (resolving `auth.uid()` → `users.supabase_uid`) are granted to `authenticated` only. Server-only RPCs (key 
read, admission/reservation, settlement, usage maintenance) are granted to `service_role` only and are called by 
server code only after the route has authorized the application user; service role never replaces that authorization. 
Integration tests call each server-only RPC directly as `anon` and `authenticated` and expect denial.
13:- **Lock order rule (review R3-1):** every meeting-notes transaction takes row locks in this one order and skips 
the ones it does not need: (1) the actor's `users` row `FOR KEY SHARE` (where rights must hold for the whole 
transaction — note mutations and the settings save; KEY SHARE still blocks the product removal and role-change RPCs, 
which take `FOR UPDATE`, and does not conflict with presence's `NO KEY UPDATE` writes to the same row — TRACK T17), 
(2) the company's AI settings row, (3) the note row `FOR UPDATE`, (4) item rows. The note row lock is the single 
per-note lock: note mutations, restore, delete, attach, item insertion (capacity), and Apply take it before touching 
items or versions. `FOR KEY SHARE` on `users` coexists with the `KEY SHARE` taken by foreign-key checks on 
version/item inserts, and still blocks the existing member-removal and role-change paths, which lock `users` `FOR 
UPDATE` before `companies` (`20260718210827_phase7_company_member_atomic_removal.sql:641`).
14:- **Online method (T15 only):** the Phase 4/5 method — re-check `npx supabase migration list --linked` and the 
target catalog immediately before; apply each reviewed file's SQL transactionally and record only its version; read 
back catalog objects; smoke-check. Never `supabase db push`, `migration up --linked`, or broad `migration repair`.
15:- **Supabase/RLS review gate (AGENTS.md):** every migration, RLS policy, grant, RPC, repository, and API route 
change passes the `supabase-rls-reviewer` audit (users.id vs supabase_uid, server client + `auth.getUser()`, service 
role never replacing authorization, function grants per the rule above) before its task is recorded done.
16:- **Adversarial review gate (AGENTS.md):** direct Codex CLI, `gpt-6.1-sol`, effort `max`, read-only, per 
`delegation-anthropic.md`; prompts and results under `reviews/`. Per block, after its last task, over the block's 
combined diff: **Block A** = T1, T17, T2, T3, T4; **Block B** = T5, T6, T7, T8; **Block C** = T9, T10, T16, T11; 
**Block D** = T12, T13; **Final** = T14 over the whole work-item diff. Another round only for a High/Medium a real 
user or realistic failure can trigger; at most one fix round per block; leftovers recorded in TRACK and carried to T14.
17:- **Verification cadence (AGENTS.md, owner decisions 2026-10-10):** per task type-check + lint (new E2E specs are 
written with their task, not run); per block, after its fix round, type-check, lint, and the DB integration suite when 
server code changed; no E2E before T14; T14 runs every new spec and the full regression E2E once in Chrome only, zero 
retries, a failing test analysed and rerun alone. One test process at a time; nothing heavy in parallel.
18:- **Test policy (SPEC §15):** integration tests in a new `__tests__/meeting-notes-db/` suite (Vitest, node env, 
local Supabase, route handlers invoked in-process — same pattern as `vitest.messaging-db.config.mts`) and Playwright 
E2E with two accounts. The external AI provider is the only thing replaced, by the local fake AI server built in T16, 
reached over real HTTP. No unit tests; a touched unit test (e.g. `__tests__/company-settings-default-space.test.tsx`, 
`__tests__/modern-space-card.test.tsx`) is replaced by integration/E2E coverage and deleted, recorded in TRACK.
19:- **Secrets:** API keys, the ticket secret, and transcript text never in logs, errors, TRACK, reviews, or reports; 
tests use canary keys only.
20:
21:## Observations grounding this plan (2026-10-13)
22:
23:- Local Supabase answers at `127.0.0.1:54321` (HTTP 200). Local Postgres (`supabase_db_virtual-office`) has 
`supabase_vault 0.3.1`, `pgcrypto`, `pg_cron`; `unaccent` and `pg_trgm` are available but not installed. 
`meeting_notes` and `meeting_note_action_items` have 0 rows locally and one SELECT policy each 
(`*_select_same_company`, joining through `spaces`); authenticated has SELECT only (baseline `20260610183139`).
24:- `meeting_notes.room_id` is NOT NULL → `spaces` ON DELETE CASCADE; `summary` NOT NULL with a non-empty check; no 
`company_id`, author, content, revision, participants, or versions; `meeting_note_action_items.due_date` is 
`timestamptz`, `assignee_id` → users ON DELETE SET NULL (baseline `20260610183000`).
25:- Baseline default privileges grant EXECUTE on new public functions to `anon` and `authenticated` 
(`20260610183000_baseline_core_schema.sql:2131-2133`).
26:- `users` has `id`, `supabase_uid`, `company_id` (nullable), `display_name`, `role`; member removal clears 
`users.company_id` (`20260718210827_phase7_company_member_atomic_removal.sql:770`). `companies.settings` is JSONB 
readable through the company SELECT policy — not usable for keys.
27:- `IMeetingNoteRepository` / `SupabaseMeetingNoteRepository` exist with no runtime caller; `useSpaceDetails` has no 
consumer. Space detail UI is `src/components/floor-plan/modern/SpaceDetailPanel.tsx` (191 lines, no tabs) and 
`SpaceDetailBottomSheet.tsx`. Company page `src/app/(dashboard)/company/page.tsx` has tabs `members` and admin-only 
`settings`. Dashboard card "AI Meeting Notes" in `QuickLinksGrid.tsx:54` has no target.
28:- Route pattern: `requireAuthUser` (`src/lib/auth/session.ts`), `createSupabaseServerClient`, 
`src/lib/auth/authorize.ts`, `enforceRateLimit` (`src/lib/auth/rate-limit.ts`). DB integration suites invoke route 
handlers in-process (`vitest.messaging-db.config.mts`).
29:- No rich-text, sanitizer, Markdown-conversion, Anthropic, or OpenAI packages are installed; `zod 4.4.3` and 
`date-fns` are.
30:- E2E harness: `playwright.messaging-phase5.config.ts` (local-Supabase mode, real Chrome channel, server log 
capture) and `__tests__/api/playwright/helpers/local-supabase-mode.ts`; two-account fixtures from the messaging suites.
31:- Online target `vhabpcoyypobgasacsko`: not inspected yet (T8 does a read-only check; T15 the full preflight).
32:- None of these contradicts the SPEC.
